# Before you start

Learn how the ISA Returns Test Support API works and what your organisation needs to consider before using the API.

## API subscription model
You must subscribe your application to the ISA Returns Test Support API before it can access the API’s endpoints.

## API access control
The ISA Returns Test Support API is a controlled access API. Endpoints are visible only to applications that are subscribed to it. To subscribe, you must sign in on the Developer Hub and request access and you will receive a confirmation email from Software Developer Support Team (SDST) with the details of the access.

## API versioning

ISA Returns Test Support API follows a simplified variation of the semantic versioning to manage changes and maintain
compatibility for API consumers. See our
[reference guide](/api-documentation/docs/api/service/disa-returns-test-support-api) for more on versioning.

Users should test and validate against the appropriate API version before deploying to production environments.

For your application to be able to call a particular version of one of our API, you must:

- confirm that the version is visible on the
[ISA Returns Test Support API’s documentation page](/api-documentation/docs/api/service/disa-returns-test-support-api)
- subscribe your application to that version
- set the correct Accept header in your API requests

Refer to the
[ISA Returns Test Support API change log](https://github.com/hmrc/disa-returns-test-support-api/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)
for all important changes and release information arranged in a reverse chronological order with the latest updates
being listed at the top of the page.

For further details on versioning, see the [Reference guide](/api-documentation/docs/api/service/disa-returns-test-support-api).

## API rate limiting

Each ISA manager must register a single application with HMRC. This application is identified during the OAuth 2.0 flow
and used for subsequent API calls.

The ISA Returns Test Support API has a rate limit of 3 requests per second per ISA manager. You cannot request an
increase.

If you exceed this limit, you will receive a 429 Too Many Requests response. Your application should pause briefly and
retry the request.

To avoid further limits:

- follow software design best practices
- avoid batching large volumes unnecessarily
- use randomised backoff timing to reduce spikes

For details on rate limiting, see the
[Reference guide](/api-documentation/docs/api/service/disa-returns-test-support-api).

## Authentication

Use your organisation’s Government Gateway user ID to authenticate with HMRC. This grants authority to your application
for the required scopes. HMRC issues an OAuth 2.0 access token, which must be included in requests to user-restricted
endpoints.

Tokens expire after four hours. You must then request a new one.

For details on user-restricted endpoints, see the
[Authorisation guide](https://developer.service.hmrc.gov.uk/api-documentation/docs/authorisation/user-restricted-endpoints).

## Error handling

The ISA Returns Test Support API uses standard HTTP status codes to indicate the outcome of a request.
Status codes are returned containing additional error information, where applicable.
Refer to the API documentation reference guide for general error-specific information.

### 5XX errors

If the API returns a 5XX error, you should retry the request. These errors are usually temporary.

Do not retry 501 Not Implemented errors. This means the endpoint does not exist or is incorrect. Check the URL.

Use an exponential backoff strategy. For example, try 3 to 5 attempts over 1 to 2 minutes. Consider using a circuit
breaker pattern.

### 4XX errors

4XX errors indicate a problem with the request. Check the error message in the response payload and fix the request
before retrying.

A 400 error may mean the payload is invalid. Check against the schema. A 406 error means the Accept header is missing
or incorrect.

If you repeatedly receive 4XX errors, contact the SDST.

Support
For support with the onboarding, the API access, or the
[HMRC Developer sandbox](https://developer.service.hmrc.gov.uk/api-documentation/docs/testing) queries, submit your
request using the [online support form](https://developer.service.hmrc.gov.uk/devhub-support/start).

When submitting a request, users should provide details of any available error information to help with the
troubleshooting and resolution of the issue.
